  <title>Unbalanced transactions</title>

  <p>Every transaction in the books should have equal debits and credits.
  <guiseq><gui>Reports</gui><gui>Financial</gui><gui>Unbalanced
  transactions</gui></guiseq> lists those that do not, so they can be found
  and repaired. Each transaction opens to show its lines. Under the list are
  how many there are, and their gross and net imbalance.</p>

  <p>The <gui>Safety</gui> column says how risky a repair is: whether the
  lines belong to a posted document such as an invoice or payment. They are
  listed safest first:</p>
  <terms>
    <item><title>Orphan</title>
    <p>No document claims any of its lines. The safest to repair in the
    ledger.</p></item>
    <item><title>Header claimed</title>
    <p>A document points to the transaction, but not to its lines.</p></item>
    <item><title>Mixed</title>
    <p>Some lines belong to a document and some do not.</p></item>
    <item><title>Attached</title>
    <p>Every line belongs to a document. Repair the document instead of the
    ledger.</p></item>
    <item><title>Empty header</title>
    <p>A transaction with no lines at all.</p></item>
  </terms>
  <p><gui>Dates</gui> flags lines dated apart from each other, and
  <gui>Reconciled</gui> those already on a bank statement.</p>

  <p>Right click a line and choose <gui>Edit GL entry</gui> to correct its
  transaction; see <link xref="edit_gl_entry"/>. <gui>Reload</gui> reads the books
  again. <gui>Report hub</gui> exports the list; see <link
  xref="report_hub"/>.</p>

</page>
